		heading: 'Verses 1-2: living close, and the decision to say it',
		bodyHtml: `<p>The psalm begins with a condition and a picture: &ldquo;Whoever dwells in the shelter of the Most High will rest in the shadow of the Almighty.&rdquo; (Psalm 91:1). &ldquo;Most High&rdquo; is <em>&lsquo;elyon</em> (Strong&rsquo;s H5945) and &ldquo;Almighty&rdquo; is <em>shadday</em> (H7706), two of the Bible&rsquo;s names for our Father&rsquo;s greatness. To &ldquo;dwell&rdquo; is to live there, not to visit. The promise is for the person who has made His shelter home.</p><p>Verse 2 is where that becomes personal: &ldquo;I will say of my Father, &lsquo;He is my refuge and my fortress, my Father, in whom I trust.&rsquo;&rdquo; (Psalm 91:2). The line is a choice spoken aloud. Trust here is not a feeling that arrives, but something said to Him and about Him.</p>`,
