		bodyHtml: `<p>The psalm then lists what the shelter covers. The first is the snare and the sickness: &ldquo;Surely he will rescue you from the hunter&rsquo;s trap and from deadly disease.&rdquo; (Psalm 91:3). The second is the image most readers remember: &ldquo;He will cover you with his feathers, and under his wings you will find refuge; his faithfulness is your shield and rampart.&rdquo; (Psalm 91:4). Our Father is pictured as a bird sheltering her young, and the weapon in His hand is His faithfulness.</p><p>Then come the fears by hour: night terror, the arrow by day, disease in the dark, plague at noon (Psalm 91:5-6). Verse 7 says, &ldquo;1,000 may fall at your side, 10,000 at your right hand, but it will not come near you.&rdquo; (Psalm 91:7). It is a poem of covering, not a guarantee of an easy life, and it has to be read beside the verses that follow.</p>`,
